WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.790
Good day, everyone.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:16.540
This is Jack Van Horn from the Big Data to
Knowledge Training Coordinating Center located

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.369
at the University of Southern California.

00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:23.140
And I'd like to welcome you to the next in
our series of Big Data to Knowledge Guide

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.010
to the Fundamentals of Data Science.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:31.040
We're absolutely delighted to have Susanna-Assunta
Sansone from the University of Oxford speaking

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.450
to us today on metadata standards.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:41.070
Susanna is the associate director at the Oxford
e-Research centre, which is a department at

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.100
the University of Oxford.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:50.690
Her research focuses on data representation,
curation management to support data reproducibility,

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:55.860
and the evolution of scholarly publishing,
which drives science as well as discoveries.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:02.370
She is the principal investigator leading
a number of programs in the UK and the European

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:09.010
Commission, as well as being a participant
in the NIH's BD2K activities.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:15.740
She's also the vice chair of the Dryad Board
and a board member of the ELIXIR UK Node,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:21.930
which is sort of a European analog of the
Big Data to Knowledge program, the Research

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.540
Data Alliance and the Force11 task force.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:31.360
She has Founding Honorary Academic Editor
of Springer Nature's Scientific Data open-access

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.390
journal.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:36.490
And today she's going to be talking about
metadata standards and interoperability of

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:41.420
standards to be able to promote operational
processes, underlying exchange, and the sharing

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:47.710
of information between different systems and
how these relate to the FAIR principles.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:52.320
That is, that data should be findable, accessible,
interoperable, and reusable.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.250
And without further ado, I'll turn it over
to Susanna.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.780
Thank you so much for joining us, Susanna.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.550
Thank you so much for having me.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.070
And good morning or good afternoon, depends
where you are.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:09.420
OK, here is the outline of my lecture today.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:16.239
I plan to actually first introduce the landscape
of standards, starting with interoperability

00:02:16.239 --> 00:02:17.239
standards.

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:25.159
And then I'll go down the area and focus on
metadata and content standards and I'll explain

00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:29.099
to you what those are, what they're useful
for.

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:35.849
And also illustrating the lifecycle of those
efforts and a few key players.

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:43.299
And I'll close to this cause actually, it's
important to have producer of the standards

00:02:43.299 --> 00:02:49.010
and consumer of the standards to both make
discoverable those resources, and for the

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:56.830
consumer to actually find the right resource
appropriate to his or her case.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:59.310
So let's start with a definition.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.310
What is a standard?

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:03.510
And, of course, this is a definition because
there are many.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:09.239
But, let's say that it's actually an agreed-upon
convention for doing something.

00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:15.790
And it can be established by an authority
or by a grassroot group.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:22.080
What I really want to define, actually, are
the interoperability standards, which are

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.719
one type of standards that are given to what
we do and what the scope of this talk is.

00:03:27.719 --> 00:03:32.279
And the best example to use, actually, it's
one of the nuts

00:03:32.279 --> 00:03:33.279
and bolts.

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:35.629
And therefore, let's use engineering.

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:40.769
Engineering in the 19th century wasn't as
easy as it is now.

00:03:40.769 --> 00:03:45.540
Now, you go and buy dry nuts that fit the
right bolts, and that's it.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.290
You know that they will work together.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:58.499
That certainly wasn't the case before standards
were defined and that was actually in the

00:03:58.499 --> 00:04:00.329
mid-- mid-19th century.

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:06.209
But those standards, although were initiated
quite early, were only adopted after the second

00:04:06.209 --> 00:04:07.329
World War.

00:04:07.329 --> 00:04:09.559
So a century after.

00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:16.400
And that's quite an interesting thing to bear
in mind, because from the development to the

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.940
adoption of standards, there is a very big
time gap.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:27.730
Now, let's talk, of course, about interoperability
standards in our area, in the life sciences

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:29.380
and biomedical sciences.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:36.390
So what we are interested in are interoperability
standards for digital object.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:44.040
Research output, being data, or codes, algorithm,
workflow, models, software, or even paper,

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.040
of course.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:51.880
Such in our cases those are also agreed upon
specification, guidance, or criteria.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:57.460
And they are designed to ensure that this,
the research object, digital research objects,

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:58.650
are FAIR.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:04.900
And FAIR actually means findable, and accessible,
and interoperable, and reusable.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.600
So interoperability standards are enablers.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:12.960
And they enable us to do better science in
a more efficient way.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.530
To actually have straightforward access and
reuse of these digital objects.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:26.000
If you're not familiar with what FAIR stands
for and what FAIR is and the work behind it

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:33.110
or what the principles say in detail, I will
actually refer you to this reading material.

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:41.970
So this is a publication in scientific data
that a group of grassroots organizations,

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:50.230
but also as you can see funders, the BD2K
or infrastructure developer and service provider

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:57.330
being ELIXIR, or other grassroots organization
and advocator, with [INAUDIBLE], Force11,

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:03.560
et cetera, have actually promoted have endorsed.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:10.280
So the FAIR principles, are really a very
precise and measurable set of guidelines that

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:18.650
allow digital object and service provider
to have infrastructure for those digital objects,

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:22.380
to make sure that those are actually FAIR,
fundable, accessible, and reusable.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:31.070
And really the third principle stressed the
fact that the digital object should be just

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:36.050
human readable, but should also be accessible
to machine.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:43.070
And the article contains a few examples of
implementations that are working towards making

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:46.930
data, all things FAIR, actually.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:50.900
Because per have certain different metrics,
metric which actually have been devised, as

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:55.720
well, by working group within the BD2K.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.780
And those are just a few examples.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.540
And as you can see there are groups within
the BD2K which are also working towards making

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:10.110
data FAIR or even standards FAIR, but I will
touch up on those as we go along in the talk.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:15.670
So let's go back to define the fundamentals
of interoperability standards.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:21.530
So, they are essential for discovering data,
or code, or software, and et cetera.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:26.770
But they're also essential to allow to site
those digital objects, and therefore give

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:34.460
credit and recognition to the person that
has actually shared and made this option FAIR.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:41.130
So we can say that, actually, the nuts and
the bolts of the interoperability standards

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.310
are metadata and identifiers.

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:49.100
So these are the essential elements to build
an interoperable machine.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:56.440
And metadata and identifiers are essential
to the underlying process, the operational

00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:02.080
process of interoperability, which will allow
us data or any other digital object to be

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:09.260
aggregated, to be integrated within another,
to be compared, to be exchanged.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:15.470
So, although, identifiers are not the focus
of this presentation, because actually it

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:21.660
requires a presentation of its own, I would
like to give you some pointers and some reading

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:27.740
material, because there is a lot of efforts
around defining unique, resolvable, and measurable

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.650
identifiers which are essential for digital
objects.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:37.900
So there are several efforts within the Force11
group, being the Resource Identification Initiative,

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:43.840
and I believe Anita Bandrowski might actually
present this in the next lecture next week.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:51.000
There are identifiers for researchers defined
by ORCID identify for data-set life for data

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.900
sites, and so on and so forth.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:09:02.670
And that is also quite nice, some of the document
which will provide you simple rules for designing,

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:07.690
and providing, and reusing persistent identifiers
and that's the reference that you can see

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:08.690
there.

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:13.140
This is a pre-print in [INAUDIBLE] and the
paper has actually been submitted.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:21.500
So, let's focus now especially on the metadata
elements, these interoperability standards.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:30.180
So, but both identifiers and metadata need
to be actually implemented by technical experts.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:35.370
So, to have a machine that works well you
need to put together the nuts and the bolts.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:37.900
And that's where you need the technical expertise.

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:45.490
You need the modelers, you need anthologists,
you need the software developers, and so on

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:46.490
and so forth.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:52.140
And we need tools, we need the registry, we
need catalogs of these digital objects.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:53.860
We need databases for data.

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:57.020
We need service around all of this.

00:09:57.020 --> 00:10:03.550
Because we need to ensure that the researcher
can actually find, can actually store, can

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:06.180
actually manage all this information.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:11.010
And they can do all they need to do in their
own research activity.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:17.310
But we need to allow them to make all this
quite seamless.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:22.131
And to do that we need to make sure that these
interoperability standards, these nuts and

00:10:22.131 --> 00:10:25.480
the bolts, are invisible to them.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:31.061
Because usually researcher has very little
knowledge and unfortunately very little interest

00:10:31.061 --> 00:10:37.020
in wanting to know what this metadata is and
what they should use, and so on and so forth.

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:44.630
So making interoperable standards invisible
is the key to their adoption and their success

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:47.730
in the end.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:51.830
So talking about metadata in particular.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:11:01.270
Now, metadata, it's a descriptor for the digital
objects that will help someone, actually,

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:08.960
to understand what that digital object is,
or where it is, or how it can be accessed,

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.930
or who owns this digital object, and so on
and so forth.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:19.740
The type of course of the descriptors that
we will find or we can use for the digital

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:21.380
object really varies.

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:25.020
Depends on
the object itself.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:31.040
And the depth and the prep, as well, will
vary depending on the type of thing we enable

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:32.720
added to those.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:39.760
So what this actually means, that the script
are for discovery, or the script are for citation,

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.310
or, for example, for credit, might be actually
different from the metadata, the one required

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:50.600
to enable reproducibility of a certain data-set.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:57.610
Reproducibility will require a richer and
deeper level of metadata.

00:11:57.610 --> 00:12:01.370
Let me give you some example now.

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:05.290
Example for software, digital object software.

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:10.350
There are several efforts out there, actually,
trying to tackle this area.

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:17.360
Especially because all the infrastructure
that supports the discovery and the presentation

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:24.120
of the software lags behind many of the other
digital objects.

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:36.110
And this is quite actually a great report
from NIH workshop, which was held in 2014.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:43.000
It's the Software Discovery Index workshop,
which really documents the need for infrastructure

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:44.520
around software.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:49.100
And some of the efforts which are in progress.

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:53.830
I want to highlight one of those efforts which
is called CodeMeta.

00:12:53.830 --> 00:13:02.370
And CodeMeta brings together academics from
around the world, and vendors, and commercial

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:06.560
entities, which are stakeholders in the area.

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:11.650
And they're working toward developing what
they call a crosswalk table that would have

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:18.010
to translate the different descriptors used
for codes and for software.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:24.850
This would certainly enhance discoverability
and reuse of software.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:33.120
There is another effort which is then focused
on the metadata for content in websites, or

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.440
in services, data services for example.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.410
And this effort is called BioSchema.

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:45.750
So BioSchema actually brings together groups
from NIH, from ELIXIR, from commercial entities

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:48.400
like Google, and many others.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:54.661
And in support of the use of schema.org which
is a structured semantic markup which is used

00:13:54.661 --> 00:13:59.620
by Google, by Bing, by Yahoo, by the major
search engines.

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:05.860
And it's coordinating extension of the scheme.org
into the life science domain.

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:12.370
This is so that data, or events, or software,
or training materials standards, which are

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:20.250
described in this, or served, in these services,
will be more discoverable by those search

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:21.250
engines.

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:28.590
And I want to flag that, of course, data,
is one of the key area and [INAUDIBLE] already

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:33.060
presented The effort by [INAUDIBLE] and the
data metadata data index.

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:35.360
Which is underpinned by the model.

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:41.730
Which is also being mapped and is expressed
with the Bioschemas.org vocabulary.

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:47.330
And because this lecture is about standards,
I got to flag as well that standard is one

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:55.330
of the content being tackled by schema.org
and BioSharing group, which I will mention

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.279
later in the talk, it's also participating.

00:14:57.279 --> 00:15:02.770
So this will ensure that also standards, which
are described in this registry, will be more

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:06.980
discoverable to widely used search engines.

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:08.920
And this is all because of the metadata.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:13.470
The common metadata from schema.org extended
to those domains.

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:20.510
So metadata standards are key to science and
I want to use the word that Mark Musen used

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:24.080
in his first lecture that opened the series.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:30.020
Well, he said that to be a data parasite,
you can only be a data parasite if you can

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:33.290
actually
access the data, you can find and access the

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:34.290
data.

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:36.860
And the data is available in standard format.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:42.930
And there is enough metadata that you can
actually understand what the data is to reuse

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:43.930
it.

00:15:43.930 --> 00:15:45.260
So that's really what metadata standards are.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:51.320
They are enablers for science to do better
science.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:59.800
The truth is, that we already know, actually,
most of the data or the digital objects, which

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:05.519
are shared, aren't exactly findable, accessible,
interoperable, usable, and for many reasons.

00:16:05.519 --> 00:16:07.450
Because the metadata isn't rich enough.

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:10.330
Because the metadata is not harmonized.

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.490
Is there atherogenicity?

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:15.840
Because things are not very well linked, they
are not very well identified, and therefore

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:16.840
they're not well cited.

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.940
Or they're not stored in the right place.

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:21.970
And so on and so forth.

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:30.149
And this is true also because those that share
those digital objects see this as a very time

00:16:30.149 --> 00:16:31.149
consuming activity.

00:16:31.149 --> 00:16:36.840
Having to add the rich metadata to describe
your work so that somebody else can reuse

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:41.310
it, it's not exactly, unfortunately, someone's
priority.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:49.400
And so curation activities are seen as some
sort of second class citizen type work.

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:55.430
Which is not the case because I want to echo
the presentation by Pascal Gaudet about biocuration

00:16:55.430 --> 00:17:02.690
and importance of professionalize the curation,
professionalize the use of standards in data

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:06.680
curation because this is essential.

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:13.030
This is just an example, one of many, that
actually you can find of importance of metadata.

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:16.030
So we share a file, we share a data file.

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:21.640
In this case, it's an excel spreadsheet with
some information in it.

00:17:21.640 --> 00:17:29.299
But certainly, just having here notation and
with a very clear manner, in an ambiguous--

00:17:29.299 --> 00:17:34.049
expressing in an ambiguous manner, it's actually
key to ensure that the data is actually FAIR

00:17:34.049 --> 00:17:35.049
at the end.

00:17:35.049 --> 00:17:40.429
This is an example when information aren't
clear and they're only clear to the person

00:17:40.429 --> 00:17:43.740
that has shared it but not to a third party.

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:48.330
Of course, this is an example where everything
is clearer and is expressed, and so on and

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:50.000
so forth.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:56.150
Let's now move from the metadata standards
to the content standards.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:18:00.860
So content standards, it's a type of metadata
standards.

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:07.120
And let me also say that this type of classification
that you see here, it's something that I put

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:15.090
together with several colleagues because there
isn't an accepted way to classify attributes

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:17.230
or types of standards.

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:20.720
But it's a simple way to show that indeed
have nested.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:27.610
So content standard is a type of metadata
standard, which is very much-- it covers descriptor

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:29.730
at the domain level.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:34.130
So, in that essential for interpretation and
essential for verification, reproducibility,

00:18:34.130 --> 00:18:35.770
reusability of a data-set.

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:46.010
I didn't mention before that a metadata for
citing a missing data-set, it's net rich than

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:52.409
metadata for interpreting, or reproducing,
when you need an eye level of detail of what's

00:18:52.409 --> 00:18:54.390
actually been done.

00:18:54.390 --> 00:19:00.529
And the breadth and depth of those descriptors
vary depending on the type of study one is

00:19:00.529 --> 00:19:01.529
actually doing.

00:19:01.529 --> 00:19:05.250
But generally, what it covers is, what was
done?

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:06.720
Who did it?

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:07.950
When was it done?

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:09.370
How was it done?

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:11.860
And, actually, why?

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:19.260
So content standards allow both experimental
components, the design, or the condition,

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:26.799
or the parameters of the experiment, the fundamental
biological entity which had been started,

00:19:26.799 --> 00:19:32.750
or any other concept which has been started,
an analytical process, or the mathematical

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:40.100
model, or any simulation that has been done,
to be harmonized with respect to structure,

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:42.179
format, and annotation.

00:19:42.179 --> 00:19:47.520
So then when we share it, we will actually
know what path is the experiment, where does

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:51.309
the design, where was the condition, which
are the parameters.

00:19:51.309 --> 00:19:57.309
So structure is essential, not just to discovery,
but to interpret, to understand, and then

00:19:57.309 --> 00:19:59.990
be able to reuse it.

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:02.980
Let's use an example, a very, very simple
example.

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:10.140
So this is just some pretext of a part of
an experiment , or a very simple one.

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:13.130
So what does it mean, structuring the information?

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:19.840
Structuring information means abstracting
the key element, the key metadata, the key

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:27.140
content metadata, out of this information
so that when you look at the text, you will

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:28.240
see the structure.

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:33.520
You'll immediately see that seven week is
the age.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:37.400
And you immediately see that the mice, it's
actually the strain name.

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:40.620
And it's the subject of this experiment.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:46.490
You immediately see that the liver it's actually
the anatomical part that has been used.

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:48.830
You immediately see where the protocols are.

00:20:48.830 --> 00:20:55.230
And you can immediately see the type of protocol
that this information is part of et cetera.

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:57.910
Now, choose another example.

00:20:57.910 --> 00:21:05.990
I do work also for Scientific Data, so it
allow me to use this as a simple example.

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:12.880
This is just a data journal where data publication
are actually anchored to structure metadata,

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:14.280
as simple as this.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:20.490
If you go on the website, on the center part
of the homepage, you were find the "isaexplorer."

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:27.030
What the isaexplorer actually is is just a
simple tool that reads and visualize the experimental

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:34.390
metadata which has been curated by a curator
editor, which is Vasha, which is there.

00:21:34.390 --> 00:21:37.620
She goes from free text to structure.

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:46.580
So that what this then implemented in the
tool will allow the user to immediately find

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:52.169
the data paper which is relevant to their
type of design that they're interested, or

00:21:52.169 --> 00:21:58.070
the type of the organ that they're interested,
or using the technology that they are interested,

00:21:58.070 --> 00:22:00.549
and/or combination of these.

00:22:00.549 --> 00:22:04.700
And I can't stress more the value of, actually,
curation.

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:10.260
And again here I have a tiny screenshot of
a Pascal presentation.

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:13.090
Because it is essential.

00:22:13.090 --> 00:22:18.431
Standards are enablers, but professionals
like curator are those that put the standard

00:22:18.431 --> 00:22:20.059
in action.

00:22:20.059 --> 00:22:26.590
Now, let's look what are the type of content
standard which are out there.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:33.490
And again, there isn't any wide agreed classification
on the content standards.

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:38.020
And this is just the way to get some structure
to what is out there.

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:41.500
I guess you count standards in three types.

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:45.600
There are guidelines, terminology, and format.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:51.720
The guidelines, starting from right-hand side,
are what other people also call minimum information

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:54.580
guidelines
checklists.

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:57.480
You might know MIAME Guidelines, for example.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:00.910
This was one of the first published in 2001.

00:23:00.910 --> 00:23:04.380
It's the Minimum Information of our Microarray
Experiment.

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:06.050
And MIAME launched a trend.

00:23:06.050 --> 00:23:10.360
And there are a lot of minimum information
now covering different domain and different

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:12.030
technology.

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:20.440
The guidelines are essential because the community
design and define what are the metadata that

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:22.660
need to be reported.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:27.650
What is the essential metadata that need to
be reported so that somebody else can actually

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:30.130
understand what was done?

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:32.130
There are terminology.

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:37.429
Now terminology widely cover control vocabulary,
taxonomy, thesauri, ontology, and so on and

00:23:37.429 --> 00:23:38.429
so forth.

00:23:38.429 --> 00:23:46.010
And they are essential because it will provide
an unambiguous identification and definition

00:23:46.010 --> 00:23:52.900
of those core information which has been reported
on gene ontology and what's an example is

00:23:52.900 --> 00:23:53.980
OVEN ontology.

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:59.549
Now I'm not going to go in more detail because
[INAUDIBLE] did an excellent job in presenting

00:23:59.549 --> 00:24:00.620
ontology.

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:06.820
The third type of a standard, it's actually
the format.

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:13.590
Those are counting, called the conceptual
model, schema, exchange format, and et cetera.

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:19.840
And these represent the structure which inter-relate
information and will allow, for example, to

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:22.160
share data from one system to another.

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:26.080
And PASTA file, it's an example of a format.

00:24:26.080 --> 00:24:32.030
There is a fourth type, actually, of standard,
which is slightly different because it's been

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:40.570
developed in a specific domain and it's driven
specific by health care industry needs.

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:49.150
And these are the common data elemental CDE,
which actually they support both patient care,

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:57.660
but also secondary use of this data, for example,
disease surveillance, populations and public

00:24:57.660 --> 00:25:01.000
health, clinical research, but also, reimbursement.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:04.690
Let's look actually now at content standard
in numbers.

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:07.550
Because there are a lot of those.

00:25:07.550 --> 00:25:16.920
I would say that more or less, there are a
thousand known guideline, terminology, and

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:17.920
format.

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:22.490
And the number counts, as you can see, from
the BioPortal and the BioSharing has resources

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:23.750
combined.

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:30.070
In terms of common data elements, taking the
NIH Common Data Elements Repository as a reference,

00:25:30.070 --> 00:25:33.830
there are almost 20,000 elements here.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:40.970
Let's now start looking a little bit at the
community which develop those standards, what

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:47.100
their motivation is, who they are, how they're
assembled, how do they create the standard?

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:48.710
What's the lifecycle of the standard?

00:25:48.710 --> 00:25:55.030
Because it's very important to understand
who produced the standard, why they're being

00:25:55.030 --> 00:25:56.270
produced et cetera.

00:25:56.270 --> 00:26:00.830
I think again here, there isn't an agreed
way to classify the groups.

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:07.360
But I divide them between a de facto standard,
which are the grassroots initiatives, like

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:12.000
the Global Alliance for Genomics and Health,
the Proteomic Standard Initiative, the Metabolomic

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:18.960
Standard Initiative, the Genomics Standard
Initiative, the imaging groups, and so on

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:20.020
and so forth.

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:24.850
Those are really bottom-up community which
have an interest in, perhaps, sharing data,

00:26:24.850 --> 00:26:26.550
sharing data between systems.

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:30.280
And the standard which they develop are, obviously,
free to use.

00:26:30.280 --> 00:26:34.620
They are mostly volunteer effort, actually,
they are all volunteer efforts.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:42.090
And they tend to have very little money to
do the work, but also to provide training,

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:43.789
et cetera.

00:26:43.789 --> 00:26:50.040
Conversely, you have the Standard Developing
Organization on the other side which are more

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:51.660
formal authority.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:56.500
The openness to participation here really
vary.

00:26:56.500 --> 00:26:58.150
Not all of them are open.

00:26:58.150 --> 00:27:03.700
You need to be member or actually nationally
nominated to be from their national presenting.

00:27:03.700 --> 00:27:08.180
It really depends on the group.

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:13.400
In many cases, the standards by those groups
are actually sold a license.

00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:16.610
And in the best case scenario, the license
at no cost.

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:19.120
But not all of them.

00:27:19.120 --> 00:27:23.000
And usually charges apply if you want training,
or you want to have a market access to the

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:24.020
standards.

00:27:24.020 --> 00:27:26.570
And that's why they're self- supportive.

00:27:26.570 --> 00:27:29.860
But that's also why the grassroots initiative
struggles with funds.

00:27:29.860 --> 00:27:31.110
Because everything is free.

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:33.240
Everything is open.

00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:37.370
It is a complex landscape out there.

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:43.730
And in order to understand what the group
does, it's also important to understand that

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:50.010
a standard be developed from this different
perspective and they have a different focus,

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:51.010
actually.

00:27:51.010 --> 00:27:55.710
There are groups which are focused on a specific
biological or clinical domain.

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:57.780
They are interested in neuroscience.

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:04.190
But there are others that come together because
they want to model processes, or there are

00:28:04.190 --> 00:28:09.330
others which are interested in bioimaging,
no matter the sample, no matter the application.

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:14.500
Some come from the biological perspective,
and other from the technological perspective.

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:19.450
The motivation for which this group come together
also vary.

00:28:19.450 --> 00:28:26.080
There are some that actually create new standard,
or they want to fill a gap that exists between

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:28.059
certain standards.

00:28:28.059 --> 00:28:33.600
Some actually come together to map, to harmonize
effort, which can be complementary or contrasting.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:35.450
Some try to extensions.

00:28:35.450 --> 00:28:40.270
Some try for repurpose the standard for other
places.

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:46.799
Also the type of people that participate in
this effort, it's really very diverse, and

00:28:46.799 --> 00:28:49.100
actually, it should be the case.

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:52.820
Because it is essential that all the stakeholders
are represented.

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:58.750
It is essential to have the technologists,
the one that bring the knowledge, the builders,

00:28:58.750 --> 00:29:04.930
the data ontologist, the modelers, et cetera,
but it's also important to have the researcher,

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:07.710
the handy users because they bring the use
cases.

00:29:07.710 --> 00:29:09.080
You need both.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:14.210
You need the consumer and the producer at
the same time.

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:19.320
And generally this group too have representative
from academia, industry, governmental, and

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:20.490
funding agency, as well.

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:28.310
And you see people that are actually involved
in the sector, and they cover different roles.

00:29:28.310 --> 00:29:31.850
But they all manage somehow digital object.

00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:37.650
Because that's the reason why you would want
to volunteer and participate in these efforts.

00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:43.909
Understanding the landscape, understanding
what's out there, how we can use content standard,

00:29:43.909 --> 00:29:48.809
what's ready to use, what needs to be further
developed, et cetera, is also been and now

00:29:48.809 --> 00:29:55.390
can be supported has been the focus of a couple
of BD2K workshop on standards.

00:29:55.390 --> 00:30:01.059
And here is the reference to one in 2013,
and one in 2015.

00:30:01.059 --> 00:30:04.900
The one in 2013 has a longer report as well
available.

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:07.980
And the other, '15, also has a report available.

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:14.850
Now, both workshop have highlighted that,
of course, standard is essential.

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:15.890
We need to build them.

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:19.429
We need to understand how to help the community
to build them.

00:30:19.429 --> 00:30:24.970
And in understanding how to help, It's also
important to highlight what the challenges

00:30:24.970 --> 00:30:27.330
are and what the pain points are.

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:32.610
And it's both a workshop to highlight that
the pain points are quite the same.

00:30:32.610 --> 00:30:35.110
And it's not just a technical problem.

00:30:35.110 --> 00:30:36.919
It's also a social problem.

00:30:36.919 --> 00:30:43.690
And social engineering is as important as
technical engineering in these cases.

00:30:43.690 --> 00:30:50.549
And they also illustrate the fact that life
cycles of the standard, they seem to be quite

00:30:50.549 --> 00:30:52.440
the same, regardless the type of organization.

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:59.850
And here, I'm going to spend a couple of slides
in explaining what the lifecycle of this standard

00:30:59.850 --> 00:31:00.850
development is.

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:02.380
There are three phases.

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:06.290
The first phase is core formulation where
actually the groups come together.

00:31:06.290 --> 00:31:08.440
The right people are assembled.

00:31:08.440 --> 00:31:15.299
But that's where it's very important to assemble
the right people to have the right expertise,

00:31:15.299 --> 00:31:18.840
the right variety and heterogeneity, as well.

00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:24.960
This is when use cases are defined, the scope
is defined, what is in and what is not.

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:28.340
What is in and what is out, it's also important.

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:31.250
And a prioritization is important.

00:31:31.250 --> 00:31:37.960
This is just an example to say that collecting
competency question, it's one of the first

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:40.770
things that this group do.

00:31:40.770 --> 00:31:45.880
And then guide them in formal questions to
decide what's in or what's not.

00:31:45.880 --> 00:31:50.510
And then from those questions, they start
to extract what are the metadata that are

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:56.940
needed and then decide how this can be structured
and represented, and et cetera.

00:31:56.940 --> 00:31:59.960
The culmination of this is followed by a development
phase.

00:31:59.960 --> 00:32:03.870
And this standard iteration is usually done
by a

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:05.350
smaller group.

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:09.340
This is especially true in grassroots organization
where there is little money.

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:14.390
So very few people that actually have the
time, the possibility to come and do the work

00:32:14.390 --> 00:32:16.200
to get it for the others.

00:32:16.200 --> 00:32:21.159
But it's also important to engage, continue
maintain engagement to put the broader community

00:32:21.159 --> 00:32:27.370
for testing for getting feedback, for having
evaluation cycles.

00:32:27.370 --> 00:32:28.970
That's essential.

00:32:28.970 --> 00:32:33.190
And it's also very important, this phase,
to be able to actually analyze the different

00:32:33.190 --> 00:32:38.950
perspective that the people working in this
path and the effort had, and what the options

00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:44.159
are, the technical option and the solution.

00:32:44.159 --> 00:32:46.860
A third phase of this life cycle is called
maintenance.

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:53.559
Now maintenance, it's when this community
start building exemplar implementation.

00:32:53.559 --> 00:32:55.890
This is the phase where you actually start
implement and making the standard invisible.

00:32:55.890 --> 00:33:01.690
You have the tool, the databases, and the
services.

00:33:01.690 --> 00:33:04.170
They make [INAUDIBLE] invisible and usable.

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:09.970
This is also the phase where it's essential
to have documentation, both technical documentation,

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:16.330
but also user guides, education material,
some metrics by which people can understand

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:20.030
if they actually meet it or not, the standards.

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:23.990
It's also phase where the committees start
thinking about how to sustain themselves,

00:33:23.990 --> 00:33:26.669
how do they move in the next phase.

00:33:26.669 --> 00:33:32.400
Because standards, it's not something that
it's done and is static.

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:35.400
It's a very dynamic thing.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:37.409
And science change.

00:33:37.409 --> 00:33:41.720
There is a new type of process to explore.

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:48.409
Or the process is actually enriched by new
information, or there is a new technology

00:33:48.409 --> 00:33:52.200
out, and therefore, standards have to be extended,
adjusted, adopted.

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:54.830
They need to evolve as science evolves.

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:56.010
There are versions.

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:58.880
And there is backwork compatibility with this
version.

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:01.260
There are conversion model and et cetera.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:02.950
But it's not a trivial thing.

00:34:02.950 --> 00:34:09.329
And backing and developing standards, it's
a kind of lifelong commitment, I would say.

00:34:09.329 --> 00:34:13.629
Let me also highlight a couple of pain points.

00:34:13.629 --> 00:34:19.970
Because it's important if you're a producer,
or if you are a consumer of the standard,

00:34:19.970 --> 00:34:25.409
to be aware of what the difficulty is if you
are building it or trying to use it.

00:34:25.409 --> 00:34:27.450
There are key issues.

00:34:27.450 --> 00:34:28.790
One is the fragmentation.

00:34:28.790 --> 00:34:31.010
There are so many activities.

00:34:31.010 --> 00:34:36.350
And this is great because they are rooted
in real needs, use cases.

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:41.020
If you are an epidemiologist, why would you
want to develop a standard for plant?

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:42.490
Now, of course you don't.

00:34:42.490 --> 00:34:47.250
You assemble the community which, like you,
has the same interest.

00:34:47.250 --> 00:34:53.260
But what actually happens that because this
community are so diverse, and they have their

00:34:53.260 --> 00:35:00.210
own environment to work, they might tend to
duplicate, unnecessary duplicate, perhaps

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:06.099
metadata elements which are common, which
are common regardless the type of biological

00:35:06.099 --> 00:35:09.849
sample you use or regarding the technology
used.

00:35:09.849 --> 00:35:12.849
And this picture is actually trying to illustrate
the problem of fragmentation.

00:35:12.849 --> 00:35:16.130
And I can go on forever to explain fragmentation.

00:35:16.130 --> 00:35:17.250
But I don't want.

00:35:17.250 --> 00:35:24.690
I think this actually does give enough of
a picture of the difficulties.

00:35:24.690 --> 00:35:29.390
There are other pain points besides fragmentation,
which is, of course, we need to coordinate,

00:35:29.390 --> 00:35:33.820
or we need to harmonize, we need to handle
extensions.

00:35:33.820 --> 00:35:37.160
How to incentivize the people that work with
you or the contributors?

00:35:37.160 --> 00:35:42.270
How do you get somebody else to come in because
you need a new expertise?

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:48.609
How do you manage governance when you have,
for example, industry to participate?

00:35:48.609 --> 00:35:50.210
Or who owns the standard?

00:35:50.210 --> 00:35:51.810
Who owns the second standard?

00:35:51.810 --> 00:35:54.070
Do you own the definition of the standard?

00:35:54.070 --> 00:35:56.150
Do you own the use case of the standard?

00:35:56.150 --> 00:35:59.580
There's no ownership, especially in grassroots
initiative.

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:04.720
But it is essential because people want some
rewards and want some credit when contributing.

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:08.320
Funding stream, it's always a problem.

00:36:08.320 --> 00:36:14.030
Although it's important to acknowledge that
especially the NIH has launched a specific

00:36:14.030 --> 00:36:15.050
funding program for standards.

00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:18.660
This is going to be essential to help the
community.

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:25.080
There is also an important pain point, which
is indicator and evaluation method.

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:30.510
It's really hard sometimes from the standard,
the standard is successful, has been successful,

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:34.980
and why has helped, and how?

00:36:34.980 --> 00:36:36.430
Implementation as you see, they're essential.

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:42.390
It's essential to make the standard invisible
so we need the tools.

00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:44.420
The outreach and engagement, it's always critical.

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:46.839
This is the social engineering part.

00:36:46.839 --> 00:36:55.000
There is another important factor here, which
is the synergy, or I would say, the lack of

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:59.940
synergy, between the effort which are around
basic research, and those that come more in

00:36:59.940 --> 00:37:03.820
the health care and clinical and medical care.

00:37:03.820 --> 00:37:06.130
There's very much of a division there.

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:07.130
It's a divide.

00:37:07.130 --> 00:37:08.540
We need a bridge.

00:37:08.540 --> 00:37:11.570
And of course, education, documentation, and
training.

00:37:11.570 --> 00:37:13.530
It's really very time consuming.

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:19.990
And especially for grassroots volunteer, which
are volunteer job, actually struggle to develop

00:37:19.990 --> 00:37:23.589
enough material, keep up with updating the
material.

00:37:23.589 --> 00:37:27.670
And of course, a business model for sustainability
will be essential.

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:32.130
And this is something which is in our heart
to actually identify.

00:37:32.130 --> 00:37:34.170
OK.

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:39.640
This brings me-- I have 14 minutes left--
and brings me to, somehow, the last part of

00:37:39.640 --> 00:37:40.640
my lecture.

00:37:40.640 --> 00:37:46.220
Well, I want to do more discuss, OK, we have
this standard.

00:37:46.220 --> 00:37:51.290
We have this wealth of standards out there,
especially content standards that I'm focusing

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:52.290
on.

00:37:52.290 --> 00:37:54.250
But I am a consumer of this standard.

00:37:54.250 --> 00:37:58.770
So where do I go to find what I need?

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:02.640
And who can help me to find what I need?

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:04.150
Or I am a producer of a standard.

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:06.890
I just produced a visible format with all
my community.

00:38:06.890 --> 00:38:13.700
And how do I make sure that my format is actually
visible to other people, which are operating

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:16.859
in the same area, that perhaps could join
forces with us.

00:38:16.859 --> 00:38:23.140
Or at least, I will make sure that they're
not duplicating it if I make it visible.

00:38:23.140 --> 00:38:28.430
Understanding the standard, understanding
the context of the standard associate this

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:32.970
essential for consumer for producer of the
standards.

00:38:32.970 --> 00:38:37.401
And these actually are, beside the question
of just the example of the question I've just

00:38:37.401 --> 00:38:39.920
said, there many others.

00:38:39.920 --> 00:38:48.859
For example, you have curator and developers
who want to use the latest version of a certain

00:38:48.859 --> 00:38:52.710
ontology to make sure that we are annotating
it with the right version.

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:59.410
Or a developer wants to create a data submission
tool and wants to know what the latest form

00:38:59.410 --> 00:39:03.190
of the database, the database their targeting
uses.

00:39:03.190 --> 00:39:08.540
They want to know if something has been deprecated
and replaced by something else.

00:39:08.540 --> 00:39:13.740
The researcher, of course, have questions
which are, in a way, simpler because they

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:20.660
just want to know which standard is good for
their toxicological data, or which standard

00:39:20.660 --> 00:39:22.550
is actually endorsed.

00:39:22.550 --> 00:39:26.250
Which standards were recommended?

00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:33.850
But we also need to bear in mind that as part
of the stakeholder here scenario, there are

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:40.450
also the founders and the journal editors,
and others being librarians or research data

00:39:40.450 --> 00:39:50.660
manager which actually are creating guidelines,
policy, to the authors, the ORDs, the research

00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:51.660
community.

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:53.099
And they also need to be guided.

00:39:53.099 --> 00:39:54.099
They also want to know.

00:39:54.099 --> 00:39:57.980
They also have the same questions, which standard
do I recommend?

00:39:57.980 --> 00:40:02.920
And this policy, is there a list which is
approved by this community?

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:05.880
Or actually, which ones have been funded?

00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:10.880
Or which one are implemented in a certain
database?

00:40:10.880 --> 00:40:11.880
OK.

00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:16.520
Let me give you a couple of direction here
where to go where you can find things.

00:40:16.520 --> 00:40:21.109
But let me also tell you that there isn't
a single place where you find everything.

00:40:21.109 --> 00:40:24.050
Because this is a landscape which is being
mapped.

00:40:24.050 --> 00:40:31.020
If you focus on ontology, well, certainly,
I would say, you go to the BioPortal Get where

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:35.040
you deposit your ontology, your files, and
you make it visible.

00:40:35.040 --> 00:40:37.940
Also because BioPortal isn't just a depository.

00:40:37.940 --> 00:40:43.810
It provides you with tools to do many other
things, mapping, annotating, and etc.

00:40:43.810 --> 00:40:48.110
If you are in ontology engineering, and you
are building an ontology, and you want to

00:40:48.110 --> 00:40:56.160
make sure that when you build it, you are
following proper guidelines, proper principles

00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:01.560
to ensure that your ontology will be a piece
of a puzzle, would be interoperable, will

00:41:01.560 --> 00:41:05.820
be orthogonal to another ontology, then I
will say the OBO Foundry is the

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:12.290
place to go to get the guidelines and to actually
then make sure that your ontology is also

00:41:12.290 --> 00:41:18.099
present in the other Foundry so that you are
part of this larger community of people creating

00:41:18.099 --> 00:41:20.990
orthogonal ontology.

00:41:20.990 --> 00:41:29.300
Now ontology, it's only one type of standards,
of content standards, as I expressed before.

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:30.300
There are formats.

00:41:30.300 --> 00:41:31.300
There are the checklists.

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:35.920
But then, there are also the tools that implement
a standard.

00:41:35.920 --> 00:41:38.450
There are the database they implement from
the standard.

00:41:38.450 --> 00:41:43.460
And I think what's important is to bring this
landscape together.

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:50.680
Because in order to tell you if a standard
is mature, one good indicator of maturity

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:53.530
could be that the database has implemented.

00:41:53.530 --> 00:41:57.470
Because it means that there's more testing,
that there is data annotated, that at least

00:41:57.470 --> 00:42:03.570
you can verify and see if that standard works
for you, if that standard has improved the

00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:07.220
quality of the reporting of that dataset.

00:42:07.220 --> 00:42:10.599
Even indicators of maturity are not being
developed.

00:42:10.599 --> 00:42:19.240
That's the reason why, also when we were pointing
to the BioSharing, which is also part of this

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:20.530
ELIXIR infrastructure.

00:42:20.530 --> 00:42:26.570
The BioSharing, it's a curated, informative,
and educational resource which links the standard,

00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:32.400
in this case, we said the moment only count
in standards, to database and to policy to

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:35.540
understand who use what.

00:42:35.540 --> 00:42:40.800
It's really to map the landscape, to monitor
the development and evolution of the standard.

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:43.760
It isn't just to say, this standard exists.

00:42:43.760 --> 00:42:46.140
Is the standard the latest version?

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:47.821
Is the committee still supporting it?

00:42:47.821 --> 00:42:48.821
Has it been implemented?

00:42:48.821 --> 00:42:50.390
Has it been used?

00:42:50.390 --> 00:42:55.560
So the BioSharing connects to the BioPortal
because it's essential decked.

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:59.280
It's the same list of terminology and ontology.

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:04.880
But this tends to format, to model, the reporting
guideline, and et cetera.

00:43:04.880 --> 00:43:07.690
And link these to databases.

00:43:07.690 --> 00:43:14.920
Another message that actually I want to bring
up it's that standards are digital objects.

00:43:14.920 --> 00:43:22.140
We have talked so-- I have talked so far,
actually-- about standards being enablers.

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:26.150
Standards enable digital object to be fair.

00:43:26.150 --> 00:43:30.680
But actually, standard are digital objects
on their own right.

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:32.220
And they also need to be fair.

00:43:32.220 --> 00:43:36.290
Standards need to be findable, at the very
least, and accessible.

00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:40.420
And of course, interoperable, and reusable.

00:43:40.420 --> 00:43:41.420
OK.

00:43:41.420 --> 00:43:46.950
Let's say that this portal's, at least, really
address the findability of the standard, accessibility

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:48.440
of the standards, specifically.

00:43:48.440 --> 00:43:50.710
This is one of those slides that nobody can
read.

00:43:50.710 --> 00:43:57.960
But the reason why I wanted to take a screenshot
of a record in the BioSharing portal they

00:43:57.960 --> 00:43:59.320
describe the standards.

00:43:59.320 --> 00:44:04.250
In this case, it's the system biology markup
language, SBML.

00:44:04.250 --> 00:44:12.950
It's because also records, also the standard
needs content standard, needs content descriptors.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:19.810
It is important to be fundable, a standard
has its own key descriptors so that people

00:44:19.810 --> 00:44:22.520
can use those descriptors to find information
they want.

00:44:22.520 --> 00:44:29.080
It is important to describe the standard to
say, whose group is creating it, for which

00:44:29.080 --> 00:44:32.710
taxonomic range is this standard applicable?

00:44:32.710 --> 00:44:34.520
What area does this standard cover?

00:44:34.520 --> 00:44:35.520
And this is really tricky.

00:44:35.520 --> 00:44:40.000
In this case, this is, of course, a [INAUDIBLE]
model, but it's also for molecular entity,

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:41.940
for pathway, and et cetera.

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:45.849
It's important to tag the standard, a way
to make it discoverable.

00:44:45.849 --> 00:44:52.040
It's important to say if there is support,
who the group is, who is maintaining the record,

00:44:52.040 --> 00:44:58.010
who is updating the record, who has funded
the standards, are there publication associated,

00:44:58.010 --> 00:44:59.500
are there predicted standard?

00:44:59.500 --> 00:45:02.329
And this is what meant mapping the landscape.

00:45:02.329 --> 00:45:05.770
Describing the standards so that's findable.

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:07.460
But doesn't just mean that.

00:45:07.460 --> 00:45:13.150
In order for a consumer to actually pick the
right one, and the "right" in quotation, I

00:45:13.150 --> 00:45:16.750
say, you need to have indicators.

00:45:16.750 --> 00:45:20.740
Now, those indicators as of now, do not exist.

00:45:20.740 --> 00:45:23.890
But this is what actually is being developed.

00:45:23.890 --> 00:45:29.430
And hopefully pretty soon there will be a
sector of indicators who help you choose.

00:45:29.430 --> 00:45:34.130
And those indicators will be richer and richer
as the community agrees on what is the right

00:45:34.130 --> 00:45:35.720
indicator to use.

00:45:35.720 --> 00:45:43.150
At the moment, the BioSharing, for example,
use only few indicators to start with.

00:45:43.150 --> 00:45:48.300
It lists, marks, what's ready for implementation,
what's in development, what's entered as certain,

00:45:48.300 --> 00:45:49.859
and what's been deprecated.

00:45:49.859 --> 00:45:54.970
And all these tags are actually vetted with
the community which is behind the standards.

00:45:54.970 --> 00:45:59.660
An uncertain status might mean that, correct,
the group is no longer active.

00:45:59.660 --> 00:46:01.880
The standard is there.

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:05.260
It's still good, perhaps, could be even used
by databases.

00:46:05.260 --> 00:46:08.810
But the community behind it is no longer active.

00:46:08.810 --> 00:46:10.990
In developments is just started.

00:46:10.990 --> 00:46:16.920
Ready to use and implementation means it's
either ready and not yet been implemented,

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:18.099
or even been implemented.

00:46:18.099 --> 00:46:25.099
Let me show an example using standards by
the LINCS group, which is part of the NIH

00:46:25.099 --> 00:46:26.099
BD2K.

00:46:26.099 --> 00:46:28.360
These are just two records.

00:46:28.360 --> 00:46:32.230
This show the LINC standard for an important
cell line.

00:46:32.230 --> 00:46:33.230
It's a reporting guide.

00:46:33.230 --> 00:46:37.300
But on the left-hand side, it's a version
which has been deprecated.

00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:43.730
So it's a version 1, deprecated, which has
been superseded by another version, 2.

00:46:43.730 --> 00:46:45.070
And this is important.

00:46:45.070 --> 00:46:48.710
Because you might have people who have used
the version 1.

00:46:48.710 --> 00:46:53.850
And they need to understand that they still
need to find that version of the standard.

00:46:53.850 --> 00:46:59.340
But they will need to be directed to use the
latest version.

00:46:59.340 --> 00:47:05.109
And when is possible, and when is known, the
BioSharing works with the community, in this

00:47:05.109 --> 00:47:11.859
case, the LINCS community, to understand the
reason for this, in this case, this deprecation,

00:47:11.859 --> 00:47:16.230
and justify the reasons so that this reason
becomes public.

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:22.000
And somebody who comes to the registry, or
come and discover the LINCS standard,

00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:26.200
knows why it's being deprecated.

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:30.680
Searching the standard can be tricky and is
tricky at the moment.

00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:34.760
Because it's important to classify them and
categorize them.

00:47:34.760 --> 00:47:38.170
And this portal, it's a work in progress,
of course.

00:47:38.170 --> 00:47:44.410
But it's also I also want to underline the
fact that there are ways to get packaged information.

00:47:44.410 --> 00:47:50.700
There are way to package a set of standards
that perhaps it's important for your domain.

00:47:50.700 --> 00:48:00.410
It's around pathway information, or it's for
clinical data, or it's for imaging, it's possible

00:48:00.410 --> 00:48:03.619
to actually package and group records.

00:48:03.619 --> 00:48:09.190
And we are working with a committee to try
to prepackage the standards so that we can

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:12.390
direct you to a group of standards for a certain
domain.

00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:15.160
and make the search and the discovery easier.

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:18.550
But I want to go one level deeper.

00:48:18.550 --> 00:48:21.580
I know the time I have four minutes in front
of me.

00:48:21.580 --> 00:48:22.690
I should actually make it.

00:48:22.690 --> 00:48:26.670
This is the last topic I will cover.

00:48:26.670 --> 00:48:27.790
It's going deeper now.

00:48:27.790 --> 00:48:34.099
What I show you here is that the three types
of standard, guidance, technology, and format

00:48:34.099 --> 00:48:35.990
actually are related to each other.

00:48:35.990 --> 00:48:43.480
There are relations that need to be built
for someone to know what guidelines is implemented

00:48:43.480 --> 00:48:44.630
by database.

00:48:44.630 --> 00:48:50.411
If there is a terminology which the guidelines
recommend, or if there is a format that the

00:48:50.411 --> 00:48:55.240
guidelines recommend, we need to actually
build this relation.

00:48:55.240 --> 00:48:58.430
And we need them discoverable so that standards
are used.

00:48:58.430 --> 00:49:04.160
It's a complex web of relation and let me
give you an example, hopefully, a pretty simple

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:05.160
one.

00:49:05.160 --> 00:49:07.990
On the left-hand side, you see two databases.

00:49:07.990 --> 00:49:16.190
I've taken the most well-known databases NCBI
GEO and EBI ArrayExpress for gene expression

00:49:16.190 --> 00:49:18.060
data and transcriptomics data.

00:49:18.060 --> 00:49:24.369
Now, both databases implement the checklist,
a reporting guide called MIAME.

00:49:24.369 --> 00:49:30.430
But actually the two databases require different
type of format for data submission.

00:49:30.430 --> 00:49:32.670
GEO implements the [INAUDIBLE] format.

00:49:32.670 --> 00:49:36.670
And not just that one, one out of many.

00:49:36.670 --> 00:49:39.690
I just want to simplify the example.

00:49:39.690 --> 00:49:43.109
ArrayExpress requires a data submission in
each tag.

00:49:43.109 --> 00:49:46.050
Now this relation needs to be built.

00:49:46.050 --> 00:49:52.230
And indeed what I'm showing to you that those
databases use more than just that standard.

00:49:52.230 --> 00:49:54.560
They use more standards.

00:49:54.560 --> 00:49:59.760
And nevertheless, they both use the same reporting
guidelines implemented in different formats

00:49:59.760 --> 00:50:02.040
expressed with different terminology.

00:50:02.040 --> 00:50:08.930
If somebody wants to-- if a developer comes
and says, I want to build a data submission

00:50:08.930 --> 00:50:12.390
to GEO, a tool that's submitted to GEO, which
is the standard that GEO uses?

00:50:12.390 --> 00:50:20.810
They will be able to actually trace back and
understand, looking at the registry, looking

00:50:20.810 --> 00:50:23.450
at these relations.

00:50:23.450 --> 00:50:29.510
A couple of slides here which seems very hard
to read, and actually are hard to read, really

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:37.880
show you how this
network relation, it's really quite complicated,

00:50:37.880 --> 00:50:40.569
although it's very useful.

00:50:40.569 --> 00:50:47.099
This just an illustration of the relation
between standard databases and policy which

00:50:47.099 --> 00:50:49.560
are recommended by different journals.

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:53.230
But the [INAUDIBLE] restricting this relation
is essential.

00:50:53.230 --> 00:51:01.400
Because as you can see from this very now
dense network graph here, what I'm showing

00:51:01.400 --> 00:51:08.410
you here it's that although there is a relation
between certain standard databases that policy

00:51:08.410 --> 00:51:13.230
recommends, there are many more standards
or in databases that, for example, a journal

00:51:13.230 --> 00:51:19.470
could recommend that a user could pick that's
relevant to he or she.

00:51:19.470 --> 00:51:24.550
But actually, they're not necessarily immediately
seen because there is so much.

00:51:24.550 --> 00:51:30.070
And the user needs to be guided, you need
to be guided, to looking for the right standard.

00:51:30.070 --> 00:51:35.740
What we are creating is a network that will
inform a guide what's really important.

00:51:35.740 --> 00:51:43.010
Let me only point to very briefly on the right-hand
side, for example, you can actually see orange

00:51:43.010 --> 00:51:51.440
dots that represent databases which are recommended
by the PLOS data sharing policy.

00:51:51.440 --> 00:51:55.470
But as you can see, those database here, these
are model databases.

00:51:55.470 --> 00:51:57.340
All of them use standards.

00:51:57.340 --> 00:52:03.160
All of them use an ontology, which is Go,
which is in gray and is just behind.

00:52:03.160 --> 00:52:11.400
By showing to the PLOS editor that actually
those databases that they recommend also use

00:52:11.400 --> 00:52:13.290
a standard will be essential.

00:52:13.290 --> 00:52:20.390
Because the PLOS policy can evolve and they
could recommend Go as a standard for the annotation.

00:52:20.390 --> 00:52:25.810
And crediting and recommendation of standard
as well and so and so broader visibility is

00:52:25.810 --> 00:52:33.839
essential to help the community to actually
keep building it, make it even more usable.

00:52:33.839 --> 00:52:39.460
I've just realized from my watch that I've
reached the 50 minutes.

00:52:39.460 --> 00:52:45.220
And I want to close with this summary slide,
which actually isn't a summary slide.

00:52:45.220 --> 00:52:50.860
It's a reading material slide which points
again to the report from the two NIH workshops

00:52:50.860 --> 00:52:58.480
which are, I think, very informative, in terms
of the standards, metadata, and community.

00:52:58.480 --> 00:53:01.520
And many people have contributed to those
workshops and the reports.

00:53:01.520 --> 00:53:08.820
I also want to point you to the FAIR paper
by Wilkinson et al., and also a report that

00:53:08.820 --> 00:53:13.210
myself and a colleague have put together as
a part of the Wellcome Trust literature review

00:53:13.210 --> 00:53:16.670
and thinkbase, which, and fortunately, is
still being made available.

00:53:16.670 --> 00:53:20.210
But this is the link.

00:53:20.210 --> 00:53:23.200
And I've been told that in a week or so will
be available.

00:53:23.200 --> 00:53:29.350
And I want to close with a message that says
that as the data science grow, digital output

00:53:29.350 --> 00:53:32.250
are being recognized as
first-class citizens.

00:53:32.250 --> 00:53:39.380
I think we need to go a further step, one
step further, and also recognize that standards

00:53:39.380 --> 00:53:44.130
and interoperability standard are digital
objects in their own right.

00:53:44.130 --> 00:53:50.470
And they have to count with their known associative
research, development, and educational activity.

00:53:50.470 --> 00:53:51.960
Thank you.

00:53:51.960 --> 00:53:54.440
Susanna, that was really outstanding.

00:53:54.440 --> 00:54:01.780
Thank you so much for this fantastic lecture
on metadata standards, and so comprehensive,

00:54:01.780 --> 00:54:04.020
really a tour de force lecture.

00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:05.020
Thank you.

00:54:05.020 --> 00:54:07.530
We do have a few questions already lined up.

00:54:07.530 --> 00:54:11.880
I'd like to encourage those of you listening
to, if you have some questions, you can use

00:54:11.880 --> 00:54:16.650
the question feature on the GoToWebinar panel
there on the, probably right-hand side of

00:54:16.650 --> 00:54:21.270
your screen, and to encourage you to submit
some questions that we'll try to get them

00:54:21.270 --> 00:54:23.410
all if we have time.

00:54:23.410 --> 00:54:28.550
The first question that has got my attention
here is what suggestions do you have, Susanna,

00:54:28.550 --> 00:54:35.630
for how to bridge the experts, the decision
makers, and the implementers in this process

00:54:35.630 --> 00:54:38.900
of standards development of use and practice?

00:54:38.900 --> 00:54:43.310
Well, the best advice, it's really what the
community are doing.

00:54:43.310 --> 00:54:49.200
They're actually bringing those stakeholder
at the table from the beginning, at formulation

00:54:49.200 --> 00:54:51.070
phase.

00:54:51.070 --> 00:54:57.640
It is essential, as I illustrated in the lifecycle,
that the formulation phase includes all stakeholders,

00:54:57.640 --> 00:55:01.790
not the ones that they will build the standards,
but also the one that they will use it and

00:55:01.790 --> 00:55:02.790
implement it.

00:55:02.790 --> 00:55:06.640
It is essential to bring them at the table
from the very beginning.

00:55:06.640 --> 00:55:08.380
It doesn't assure adoption.

00:55:08.380 --> 00:55:11.970
But it will certainly give the best shot.

00:55:11.970 --> 00:55:16.500
Yeah, I would say that that is a very important
element of this.

00:55:16.500 --> 00:55:23.119
Otherwise, you run this danger of a "not invented
here" syndrome amongst some people who may

00:55:23.119 --> 00:55:26.580
feel that they got alienated somehow.

00:55:26.580 --> 00:55:34.020
One other question is, can you talk about
taking ontologies and standards and making

00:55:34.020 --> 00:55:37.760
them computable using the semantic web?

00:55:37.760 --> 00:55:38.760
Yeah.

00:55:38.760 --> 00:55:39.760
Definitely.

00:55:39.760 --> 00:55:45.990
Semantic web technology is widely used by
many of this standard community.

00:55:45.990 --> 00:55:48.440
When I say format, as I say, this is really
broad.

00:55:48.440 --> 00:55:52.690
There are standard which are expressed in
RDF, for example.

00:55:52.690 --> 00:55:58.790
So absolutely.

00:55:58.790 --> 00:56:05.220
When one has data dictionaries for metadata
standards, these sort of go hand-in-hand.

00:56:05.220 --> 00:56:14.920
Is there any sort of implication about reproducible
science that one can draw from the role of

00:56:14.920 --> 00:56:19.809
having data dictionaries, linking them with
standards, and then using those to help promote

00:56:19.809 --> 00:56:20.809
reproducible science?

00:56:20.809 --> 00:56:22.059
Does that make sense?

00:56:22.059 --> 00:56:24.369
What is definition of data dictionary?

00:56:24.369 --> 00:56:27.079
I think we all have different definition.

00:56:27.079 --> 00:56:31.220
For having-
You're going to need a dictionary to keep

00:56:31.220 --> 00:56:34.369
track of all the terms for data dictionaries
and stuff.

00:56:34.369 --> 00:56:40.829
I think it's just really the list of-- it's
almost in itself-- it's almost a conflated

00:56:40.829 --> 00:56:43.859
concept, in the sense that you've got a data
element.

00:56:43.859 --> 00:56:47.910
And it has, say, a range or data type.

00:56:47.910 --> 00:56:50.550
And these are allowed values.

00:56:50.550 --> 00:56:55.700
If it's a missing value, it might hold this
sort of a value.

00:56:55.700 --> 00:57:02.960
On one hand, it's almost synonymous with an
ontology, or some sort of metadata standard,

00:57:02.960 --> 00:57:06.211
but perhaps, is there a subtle distinction
there?

00:57:06.211 --> 00:57:08.579
Or are we really talking about the same thing?

00:57:08.579 --> 00:57:11.450
Well, I think there is such a subtle distinction.

00:57:11.450 --> 00:57:18.921
And to be honest, the classification that
are shown about guideline, terminology and

00:57:18.921 --> 00:57:24.040
format, it's really just a way to try to classify
things.

00:57:24.040 --> 00:57:30.230
Because I have colleagues that argue that
ontology are, have, a structure, have a format.

00:57:30.230 --> 00:57:31.320
Of course they are.

00:57:31.320 --> 00:57:32.640
They're expressing a format.

00:57:32.640 --> 00:57:33.640
They're expressing now.

00:57:33.640 --> 00:57:36.540
They're expressing a novel format.

00:57:36.540 --> 00:57:41.430
I think it's very, very hard to try to box
set these things.

00:57:41.430 --> 00:57:45.480
Because there are a lot of different ways
to structure the information.

00:57:45.480 --> 00:57:48.780
And it's hard to say which is the right way.

00:57:48.780 --> 00:57:54.309
The problem is because there's such a variety
of ways to structure and express the standards

00:57:54.309 --> 00:57:57.240
that there is a lot of fragmentation as well.

00:57:57.240 --> 00:58:00.780
But it's also essential-
I think-

00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:01.780
Yeah.

00:58:01.780 --> 00:58:06.660
Someone once said that the best thing about
standards is that there's a lot of them.

00:58:06.660 --> 00:58:10.130
In a sense, I don't know.

00:58:10.130 --> 00:58:14.440
There is a variety of them.

00:58:14.440 --> 00:58:19.099
And people will often vote with their feet
on which one they seem to like and it does

00:58:19.099 --> 00:58:20.550
the best job.

00:58:20.550 --> 00:58:24.350
And it gets also to the point of versioning
of standards.

00:58:24.350 --> 00:58:29.190
And I'm sure you probably touched on this,
at least in brief, or in part.

00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:35.150
And I'm wondering how that factors into the
evolution of standards and how important that

00:58:35.150 --> 00:58:41.369
is and if you wanted to comment?

00:58:41.369 --> 00:58:42.560
It's actually essential.

00:58:42.560 --> 00:58:43.560
Essential.

00:58:43.560 --> 00:58:47.740
Well, I would say that probably versioning,
in terms of practice, it's used especially

00:58:47.740 --> 00:58:50.740
in the ontology, in the ontology world.

00:58:50.740 --> 00:58:53.040
But it's also true in terms of formatting,
as well.

00:58:53.040 --> 00:58:58.579
Again, here the practice is not widespread
and it's still heterogeneous, depending on

00:58:58.579 --> 00:59:02.290
the community and depending on their approach.

00:59:02.290 --> 00:59:03.970
Right.

00:59:03.970 --> 00:59:07.319
Let's see.

00:59:07.319 --> 00:59:16.650
Are there any other questions here?

00:59:16.650 --> 00:59:21.890
One question is, one last one is are there
any emergence of metadata standards which

00:59:21.890 --> 00:59:27.869
have been developed specifically for computational
sciences that you might be aware

00:59:27.869 --> 00:59:28.869
of?

00:59:28.869 --> 00:59:29.869
Computational sciences.

00:59:29.869 --> 00:59:35.360
I'll refer you especially to the "Combined"
group.

00:59:35.360 --> 00:59:38.160
Combined brings together computational modeling,
system biology.

00:59:38.160 --> 00:59:39.660
It's called Combined.

00:59:39.660 --> 00:59:48.800
It's actually combined and brings the right
type of standard that perhaps this person

00:59:48.800 --> 00:59:49.930
is interested.

00:59:49.930 --> 00:59:55.880
Otherwise, please contact me, and I'll try
to actually point you to the right one.

00:59:55.880 --> 00:59:56.980
That sounds wonderful.

00:59:56.980 --> 01:00:00.280
Well, everyone, we are at the top of the hour
now.

01:00:00.280 --> 01:00:07.670
And I'd like to thank Susanna once again for
a really fantastic and rich survey of metadata

01:00:07.670 --> 01:00:09.410
standards and all that that entails.

01:00:09.410 --> 01:00:15.980
I want to thank you all for joining us this
morning and to once again tune in next week,

01:00:15.980 --> 01:00:19.660
as they say, for our next in our series of
data science seminars.

01:00:19.660 --> 01:00:21.050
Enjoy your weekend.

01:00:21.050 --> 01:00:22.520
Thank you again.

01:00:22.520 --> 01:00:24.410
Thank you for having me.

01:00:24.410 --> 01:00:25.490
Thank you.

01:00:25.490 --> 01:00:27.109
Thank you Susanna.

